WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:34.500
The Kurds are the largest ethnic minority
in the world who do not have a state of their own.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:49.580
There are approximately 30 million Kurds in Syria, Iraq, Iran and Turkey.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.120
They have now become very important actors

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.400
not only in Iraq, but also in the Syrian civil
war and in Turkey.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.619
The Kurdish people are separate, they are
linguistically different, they are culturally different

00:01:03.619 --> 00:01:10.960
from the dominant group in each one of these countries, which are Arab, Turkish, and Persian.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:17.640
The Kurdish people are mostly Muslims, they are moderate, they are secular, they are pro-Western.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.980
If you ask every Kurd, they have this dream of having a greater Kurdistan.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:28.490
And yet, because they were divided at the
end of the First World War when the

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:33.560
Ottoman Empire collapsed and the borders were drawn at the time by both colonial powers and by

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:38.980
the Turks, and because they were a minority in each of these countries they never succeeded

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.320
in getting their own state.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.080
However, the Kurds never accepted the conditions

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:50.420
in which the international community or history put them and always rebelled.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:57.230
But because they rebelled over the years they were always a threat to the stability of all these countries.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:04.700
The Kurdish question is for the Kurds, this
long-term quest to establish their own independence

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:10.200
from the countries around them. They, to date, have not succeeded in doing this because

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.200
of the geopolitics in the region.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.680
In none of these countries, when you look

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:26.920
at the history, Kurdish cultural or political
identity was recognized. Brutal means of suppression

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:33.180
and oppression were deployed to forcibly assimilate the Kurds.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.860
Conditions on the ground are changing everywhere.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.500
The Kurds have now become very important actors thanks to three major developments.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:05.200
One is the Iraqi Kurds have achieved a great deal of autonomy in Iraq and it is autonomy

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.620
that is recognized internationally, it is
recognized by Baghdad.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:15.240
In Syria, we now see that the Syrian Kurds
have succeeded in fighting off the Islamic

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.510
state, that has taken over large chunks of
territory both in Syria and Iraq.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:26.400
And the PKK, the Turkish-Kurdish insurgent group, are now in the process of historic

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.459
negotiations with the Turkish government about some kind of resolution to the Turkish-Kurdish

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:40.359
civil war which started in 1984 and has been quite ferocious, 40,000 people died, mostly Kurds.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:49.920
The issue of the Kurds, and the Iraqi Kurds

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:56.680
in particular, has become a very serious consideration for US foreign policy because they played

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:02.370
a really prominent role in helping create
the post-Saddam Iraq.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:08.180
And now, we see that the Kurds have been a partner in trans-national issues,

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.240
particularly in the fight against the Islamic State.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.060
The Kurds of Northern Iraq have been essentially

00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:18.829
autonomous from Baghdad from the time the first Gulf War ended in 1991, and the United

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:23.430
States and allies sought to protect the three provinces that make up the Kurdistan Regional

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:27.630
Government, and has provided an opportunity for the Kurds to develop their own society.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:31.640
Since the fall of Saddam Hussein at the hands of American forces, there has been this effort

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:37.800
to create a unified federal Iraq, but giving
the Kurds a special status as a region within Iraq.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:44.660
It is difficult for us to keep waiting for an uncertain future,

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:50.760
waiting in vain for implementation of the constitution and our rights.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:56.420
That's why Kurds have no choice but to think of another path, toward self-determination.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.800
The Islamic State, has erased the modern Middle East borders in a way that no other

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:10.380
state or non-state actor or force has been able to do since the collapse of the Ottoman Empire.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.880
And for the first time, because of ISIS

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:20.530
the central government in Syria and Iraq,
primarily, are very, very weak to prevent

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:23.450
the Kurds from achieving what they want.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:28.300
When the Syrians started the uprising against

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:34.259
president Bashar al-Assad's regime, Assad turned a blind eye to the Kurds, so the Kurds

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:39.459
exploited that opportunity. They declared
self rule in northern Syria.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:47.320
When the Islamic State swept through Syria, the Syrian Kurds put up a resistance, which

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:52.880
essentially got the Americans bombing the ISIS positions.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:03.220
In Turkey the Kurdistan Workers' Party, which is a terrorist organization, had been fighting

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:08.550
against the Turkish state since the mid 1980s seeking their own independent state or at

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:14.490
least autonomy from the Turkish state. But since 2002 when Erdogan and others came to

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:18.400
power, there has been, some opening to the Kurds.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.780
Erdogan has sought a negotiated solution to the problem of the PKK.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:30.770
"This normalization process will be to the
benefit of all of our citizens, Turkish, Kurdish, and other ethnicities."

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.980
"I invite the PKK to attend an extraordinary

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:49.610
congress in spring to make the strategic and historic decision to abandon the armed struggle."

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:59.660
There are dilemmas for all of the Kurds as
a result of what's happening.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.840
The major policy dilemma is related to the Iraqi Kurds. Do they stay or do they go?

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:12.520
Clearly the population very much wants independence. The fear in Ankara, the fear from the Syrian leadership,

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:21.090
the fear from the Iranian leadership, is that an independent Kurdistan would result in Kurds

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:24.090
in these other countries seeking to emulate that.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:31.600
What's complicating for U.S. strategic and
political interest is there's a very delicate

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.480
balance here both with Baghdad and with Ankara.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:40.680
The Turks are still opposed to the idea of an independent Kurdish state.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.900
However, they have come to be key economic partners for

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:51.300
the Kurds, and that has made things easier for the United States and Europe, and others,

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:56.509
in dealing with the Kurds as legitimate and important political actors.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:02.300
And in Baghdad, particularly because of this tenuous sharing of political power and sharing

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:09.210
of resources that Baghdad and the Kurds have never really been able to resolve definitively,

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:15.370
it's been difficult for the United States
to support the Kurds in the robust fashion

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:16.761
the Kurds feel they deserve.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.352
ISIS has required the United States to get

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:28.640
more deeply involved with the Kurds because the peshmerga are essentially the ground forces

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.680
in the fight against the Islamic State.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:33.620
You obviously have the fragmentation of Syria.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:38.860
You have the fragmentation of Iraq going on. Ultimately, Washington may be confronted with

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:43.610
a situation where it would be in the interests of the United States to be more supportive

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:47.210
of at least Iraqi Kurdish independence.

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:55.940
The Kurds are closer. They're not close, but they're

00:08:55.940 --> 00:09:02.180
closer to becoming independent, as the situation around them deteriorates.

